=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported symptom — laptop0 (172.16.20.10) unable to reach machines on the 172.16.10.x side such as PC4 (172.16.10.4) — does not exist in the live lab. All measured connectivity is fully functional.

The inter-VLAN routing path is correctly assembled: switch1 places laptop0's port (eth7) into br_vlan20 and the PC ports (eth1/eth2) into br_vlan10; mswitch1 trunks VLAN-tagged frames on the link to router0 via 802.1Q sub-interfaces (eth4.10 and eth4.20); router0 terminates those VLANs as SVIs eth0.10 (172.16.10.1/24) and eth0.20 (172.16.20.1/24) and performs inter-VLAN routing with ip_forward enabled. There are no iptables rules on any device in the path, no ACL, and no route filter that would block 172.16.20.x ↔ 172.16.10.x traffic.

Direct evidence: ping from laptop0 to pc4 returned 3/3 replies at TTL=63 (one routing hop, as expected); the reverse path pc4 → laptop0 also returned 3/3. Pings to pc5 (172.16.10.5) and laptop2 (172.16.10.12) likewise succeeded 3/3. Router0's ARP neighbour table lists all four hosts as REACHABLE. Laptop0 can reach the gateway 172.16.10.1 directly, and its own ARP cache confirms the MAC for 172.16.20.1 is resolved.

The helpdesk ticket describes a fault that is not present in the network. No corrective action is required; the user may be experiencing a transient host-level issue (local firewall, stale ARP on their workstation) unrelated to the network infrastructure.
=== END ===